@using global::Fluxor
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Routing
@using RonSijm.Blazyload
@using RonSijm.Blazyload.Components
@using RonSijm.Syringe
@using RonSijm.Demo.Fluxor.BobsBurgers.Redux
@using RonSijm.Demo.Fluxor.WonderWharf.Contracts
@using RonSijm.Demo.Fluxor.WonderWharf.Models
@inherits ViewModelComponent<RestaurantViewModel>
@attribute [BlazyComponent("fluxor.bobsburgers.dashboard")]
@inject IStore Store
@inject IState<AssembliesLoadedState> NativeLoads

<LayoutView Layout="@typeof(RestaurantLayout)">
<article class="fluxor-demo" data-testid="fluxor-dashboard">
    <header>
        <p class="eyebrow">Bob's Burgers / Components + Fluxor</p>
        <h1>Load a feature. Keep the store.</h1>
        <p>Wonder Wharf brings its own UI, state, reducers, effects, service and middleware into this already-running app. Bob's Burgers knows only the generated public names, request/selection models and application event.</p>
        <p class="demo-mindset" data-testid="fluxor-demo-mindset"><strong>Intentionally over-engineered.</strong> Two events do not need this much architecture. Treat this screen as about 10% of a larger project: the point is a repeatable foundation for independently developed features, not the shortest way to draw this calendar. Simple remains the minimal example.</p>
        <pre data-testid="fluxor-consumer-code"><code>@("<BlazyComponent Name=\"@WonderWharfComponents.Events\" Parameters=\"@_parameters\" />\n<BlazyComponent Name=\"@WonderWharfComponents.Summary\" />")</code></pre>
        <div class="buttons">
            <button data-testid="fluxor-show" disabled="@(_showCalendar || ViewModel.PreloadPending)" @onclick="ShowCalendar">Load / mount Wonder Wharf by name</button>
            <button data-testid="fluxor-hide" disabled="@(!_showCalendar)" @onclick="() => _showCalendar = false">Dispose both components</button>
        </div>
        <div class="preload-controls" data-testid="fluxor-preload-controls">
            <h2>Or let Fluxor request the feature first</h2>
            <div class="buttons">
                <button data-testid="fluxor-preload-assembly" disabled="@(WharfLoaded || _showCalendar || ViewModel.PreloadPending)" @onclick="() => Dispatcher.Dispatch(new PreloadWharf(false))">Preload with LoadAssembly</button>
                <button data-testid="fluxor-preload-path" disabled="@(WharfLoaded || _showCalendar || ViewModel.PreloadPending)" @onclick="() => Dispatcher.Dispatch(new PreloadWharf(true))">Preload with LoadAssemblyForPath</button>
            </div>
            <p>These actions load and register the feature without mounting its components or requesting event data. Then use the name-based mount button. Reload the browser to compare the other cold-load path.</p>
            <pre><code>@("Dispatcher.Dispatch(new LoadAssembly(\"RonSijm.Demo.Fluxor.WonderWharf.wasm\"));\nDispatcher.Dispatch(new LoadAssemblyForPath(\"fluxor/wharf\"));")</code></pre>
            @if (ViewModel.PreloadPending)
            {
                <p role="status">Fluxor requested the feature; waiting for Blazyload's assembly-loaded notification.</p>
            }
        </div>
    </header>

    <section class="diagnostics" data-testid="fluxor-diagnostics">
        <h2>The running app, not a diagram</h2>
        <p>Store instance: <strong data-testid="fluxor-store-id">@Store.GetHashCode()</strong></p>
        <p>Wonder Wharf assembly: <strong data-testid="fluxor-assembly-status">@(WharfLoaded ? "Loaded" : "Not loaded")</strong></p>
        <p>Wonder Wharf Fluxor feature: <strong data-testid="fluxor-feature-status">@(Store.Features.ContainsKey("Fluxor.WonderWharf") ? "Registered" : "Absent")</strong></p>
        <p>Components: <strong data-testid="fluxor-mount-status">@(_showCalendar ? "Requested / mounted when ready" : "Not mounted")</strong></p>
        <p>Registered feature names: <code data-testid="fluxor-features">@string.Join(", ", Store.Features.Keys)</code></p>
        <p>Bob's own Fluxor selection: <strong data-testid="fluxor-bob-selection">@(ViewModel.SelectedEvent?.Name ?? "None")</strong></p>
        <p>Public application event: <strong data-testid="fluxor-bob-broadcast">@(ViewModel.BroadcastEvent?.Name ?? "None")</strong> (<span data-testid="fluxor-public-event-count">@ViewModel.PublicEventCount</span> received)</p>
        <p>Preload strategy: <strong data-testid="fluxor-preload-strategy">@(ViewModel.PreloadStrategy ?? "Component-name request")</strong></p>
        <p>Blazyload.Fluxor action-result state: <code data-testid="fluxor-native-loads">@string.Join(", ", NativeLoads.Value.LoadedAssemblies.Select(assembly => assembly.GetName().Name))</code></p>
    </section>

    @if (_showCalendar)
    {
        <div class="producer-components">
            <BlazyComponent Name="@WonderWharfComponents.Events" Parameters="@_parameters" />
            <BlazyComponent Name="@WonderWharfComponents.Summary" />
        </div>
    }

    <ActionTimeline />

    <section id="fluxor-benefits" data-testid="fluxor-library-guide">
        <h2>What each library buys you</h2>
        <p>This is also a tech demo for the integrations. The extra components have jobs: the layout composes state, the command bar dispatches intent, lifecycle effects own browser work, and the producer owns its own workflow.</p>
        <div class="library-grid">
            <article>
                <h3>Fluxor: searchable, shared workflows</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li>Request, success and failure actions make asynchronous work explicit.</li>
                    <li>Immutable reducer output is independently testable; two components read one state.</li>
                    <li>The selection callback stays parent/child communication. The publish button sends an intentionally public application action.</li>
                    <li>The timeline shows action order in Release. Redux DevTools adds payloads, snapshots and history in Debug.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>RonSijm.Blazyload.Fluxor</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>UseFluxor</code> keeps one provider/store startup path.</li>
                    <li><code>LoadAssembly</code> makes preloading an observable action/effect workflow.</li>
                    <li><code>LoadAssemblyForPath</code> resolves the host's logical path-to-assembly mapping.</li>
                    <li><code>AssemblyLoaded</code> lets Bob project availability without reading producer state. The lazy feature's behavior joins the existing store.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>RonSijm.Fluxor.Blazor.Web.Extensions</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>ViewModelComponent</code> supplies current state, dispatch and managed subscriptions.</li>
                    <li><code>FluxorDispatcherComponent</code> supplies dispatch to the stateless command bar.</li>
                    <li><code>ViewModelLayout</code> lets application chrome follow its own aggregate ViewModel.</li>
                    <li>Initialization loads calendar data; first-render orchestration initializes browser interop. <code>PageEffect</code> filters by component and phase.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
            <article>
                <h3>RonSijm.Syringe.Fluxor</h3>
                <ul>
                    <li><code>[Inject]</code> properties work on effects for HTTP, JavaScript, state, logging and navigation dependencies.</li>
                    <li><code>[ReduceInto]</code> projects Bob's restaurant/preferences state into the shell without duplicate reducers.</li>
                    <li>Dynamic registration attaches method reducers, reducer classes, typed effect classes and middleware.</li>
                    <li>Lazy <code>[EffectMethod]</code> wrappers join the existing store and receive injected state. The producer's publish button uses that method-based style alongside its typed HTTP effect.</li>
                    <li>The producer can attach more reducers and instance/static method effects after loading. Its optional tracking controls demonstrate preparation failure, corrected retry and repeat registration without duplicate handlers.</li>
                    <li>The runtime and component tests use the same provider factory, so injection behavior is not replaced by a simpler test-only setup.</li>
                </ul>
            </article>
        </div>
        <p>We deliberately do not add fragile storage persistence or effect-unloading tricks. A foundation should expose the tradeoffs, not turn every optional package into a dependency.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Follow one request</h2>
        <ol>
            <li><code>BlazyComponent</code> resolves the name from the catalog; Blazyload downloads the feature.</li>
            <li>The bootstrap uses <code>AddFluxorLibrary</code> to scan its assembly and register its services. Optional publication tracking has a separate assembly and is deliberately registered later.</li>
            <li>Syringe adds the feature, attached reducers, effects and middleware to the existing store before loading returns.</li>
            <li><code>ViewModelComponent</code> subscribes to state and dispatches an initialization lifecycle action.</li>
            <li>A lifecycle effect dispatches <code>LoadWharfEvents</code>. A property-injected effect calls the HTTP service, then dispatches success or failure.</li>
            <li>Reducers return new state. The calendar and summary re-render from that same state.</li>
        </ol>
        <pre><code>@("builder.UseBlazyload(options => options.UseFluxor(...));\nbuilder.Services.AddBlazyloadComponents();\n// App.razor: <Fluxor.Blazor.Web.StoreInitializer />")</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>What changes compared with Simple and Extensive?</h2>
        <p>Simple demonstrates name-based composition with little setup. Extensive adds generated contracts, services and assets. This example keeps that UI boundary and puts feature behavior in Fluxor: actions describe intent, pure reducers update state, effects perform HTTP work, and middleware observes dispatches.</p>
        <p><code>RonSijm.Blazyload.Fluxor</code> connects loading to Fluxor. <code>RonSijm.Syringe.Fluxor</code> supplies dynamic registration and effect property injection. <code>RonSijm.Fluxor.Blazor.Web.Extensions</code> supplies ViewModel components and lifecycle actions. No <code>Components.Fluxor</code> adapter is needed.</p>
        <p><strong>Disposal is not unloading.</strong> Hide the components, then show them again: selection and load count remain. Switch to another demo and return: component instances are new, but Fluxor state stays. Refresh the browser to start a new store. There is no local/session-storage persistence.</p>
        <p>Open DevTools Network and filter for <code>RonSijm.Demo.Fluxor.WonderWharf</code>. Before the first request, its implementation is absent. Contracts may already be loaded. Debug builds also connect to the Redux DevTools browser extension; Release builds do not enable it.</p>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/RonSijm/RonSijm.Blazyload.Components/tree/main/Examples/Fluxor">Read the setup and package responsibilities</a>.</p>
    </section>
</article>
</LayoutView>

@code {
    private bool _showCalendar;
    private IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object?> _parameters = new Dictionary<string, object?>();
    private bool WharfLoaded => ViewModel.LoadedAssemblies.Contains(WonderWharfFeature.AssemblyName);

    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        base.OnInitialized();
        _parameters = new Dictionary<string, object?>
        {
            ["Model"] = new CalendarRequest("Bob's Burgers"),
            ["EventSelected"] = EventCallback.Factory.Create<WharfEventSelection>(this, selection => Dispatcher.Dispatch(new RecordWharfSelection(selection)))
        };
    }

    private void ShowCalendar()
    {
        _showCalendar = true;
    }
}
